    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "row",
      "title": "How to read this dashboard",
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": { "h": 1, "w": 24, "x": 0, "y": 43 },
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Stage semantics and missing metrics",
      "description": "Explains why the three stages are not the same number.",
      "datasource": { "type": "prometheus", "uid": "prometheus" },
      "gridPos": { "h": 9, "w": 24, "x": 0, "y": 44 },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**The three stages are different events. Do not add them together or read one as proof of another.**\n\n| Stage | Metric | What it proves |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| API | `payment_http_requests_total{route=\"POST /api/v1/transfers\",status_code=\"201\"}` | The ledger transaction committed. The client got an answer. |\n| Outbox | `payment_outbox_events_published_total` | RabbitMQ confirmed the `transfer.completed` message. |\n| Consumer | `payment_consumer_messages_processed_total` | The notification was recorded for the event. |\n\nA transfer can be accepted by the API and still sit in the outbox, and an event can be published and still fail in the consumer. Watch the gap between the stages, not a single total.\n\n**Not charted because the metric does not exist:**\n\n- There is no dedicated `transfers_completed` counter. The API panel is HTTP-level and also counts idempotent replays that returned 201, so it is not a count of distinct transfers.\n- Dead-letter queue *depth* is not exported. Only the dead-letter rate is available, so backlog size must be read from RabbitMQ directly.\n- PostgreSQL pool maximum is not exported by the collector, so pool headroom cannot be charted.\n- Balances are never derived from Prometheus. Ledger state belongs to PostgreSQL."
      }
    }
  ]
}
